 "division": null,
 "division_note": "the printed heading reads `by the Marshal of the ____ District (or Territory) of` with `Illinois` written into the blank in the enumerator's hand and the district blank left unfilled. No enumerator's name is written on the exposure, so no division is recorded; 33S7-9YYJ-5D carries `S. W. Sherman` and this sheet does not, and it is not carried over from a neighbouring image.",
 "state": "Illinois",
 "county": "Cook",
 "reading": "scan_verified",
 "read_pass": "continuation cells",
 "lines_ruled_with_an_entry": 31,
 "line_count_note": "coverage.json inventoried this image at 31 lines with an entry, and read line by line it carries 31: a grid of pitch 75.79 px whose first line stands at y660 and whose thirty-first at y2934, with the body's closing rule at y2959. The grid was fitted to the INDUSTRY column's own ink — the seven manufactures-and-trades entries, the only written industry cells on the sheet, and the column that closes exactly against its printed footing — and the TOTAL column was then read against it rather than used to build it, so the row index and the numbers are independent. `tools/fit_census_line_grid.py` on those seven anchors answers 27 lines below the first anchor at rms 3.72, pitch 75.79, against rms 9.72 for the next-best line count and 11.45 for the one below — a factor of two and a half either way; the first manufactures entry stands on the sheet's SECOND line, so the fitted grid extended one line up and four down to the closing rule gives 31. Every one of the 31 slots so predicted carries ink in the TOTAL column and no slot carries two entries: a connected-component pass over the column finds exactly 31 glyph groups between the heading and the closing rule, each within 33 px of its predicted line, and none wider than 76 px — so every figure on this sheet is a single digit and none was missed for being a two-figure number read as one.",
 "form_note": "The right (continuation) sheet of the 1840 schedule. Columns as printed, left to right: twelve SLAVES columns (six male, six female age bands); TOTAL — number of persons in each family; seven NUMBER OF PERSONS IN EACH FAMILY EMPLOYED IN columns (mining, agriculture, commerce, manufactures and trades, navigation of the ocean, navigation of canals lakes and rivers, learned professions and engineers); PENSIONERS FOR REVOLUTIONARY OR MILITARY SERVICES (names, ages); six DEAF AND DUMB, BLIND AND INSANE WHITE PERSONS columns; four of the same for coloured persons; and seven SCHOOLS, &c. columns.",
 "geometry_note": "the leaf is skewed on the exposure, and in the opposite sense to 33S7-9YYJ-B1: the printed vertical rules drift about 20 px to the LEFT between y=650 and y=2890, a slope of -0.0089 px per px of y, measured as a rule-position profile in seven bands down the page (nine rules tracked, drift -23 to -18 px). The reading was made against a sheared copy (x' = x + 0.0089*(y-650), y untouched), on which the rules of the TOTAL and industry blocks stand at x = 1345, 1538, 1608, 1670, 1736, 1817, 1898, 1977 and 2041 at every height to within 3 px — a TOTAL column 193 px wide and industry columns 70, 62, 66, 81, 81, 79 and 64 px, against B1's 195 and 66, 64, 65, 81, 80, 81, 65, which is the same printed form measured on a slightly larger exposure. Every x quoted in this file is an x on that sheared copy; every y is a y of the exposure itself, because a horizontal shear leaves y alone.",
 "tool_note": "tools/read_census_continuation.py still does not run on this image: 33S7-9YYJ-BF is one of the four its rule finder exits on, for the skew recorded above. The reading here was made at magnification against a measured, sheared column map, on the same basis as 33S7-9YYJ-8D and 33S7-9YYJ-B1. The tool is not weakened to fit.",
 "right_edge_note": "The exposure goes black at x' ≈ 3850 where the leaf curves into the gutter. The form's own last column — white persons over 20 years of age who cannot read and write, which stands to the right of the seven SCHOOLS, &c. columns and is not one of the 37 this schema carries — is lost in that black, exactly as it is on 33S7-9YYJ-B1 and 33S7-9YYJ-24, and it is not recorded here in either direction: the sheet does not say. The seven SCHOOLS, &c. columns themselves are legible and were read; they are empty on every one of the 31 lines and empty at the foot.",
 "closure_note": "The enumerator added his own totals at the foot of the sheet. Those totals are transcribed under `footer_as_read` and every column carrying a figure is checked against them in `column_closure`. A column that does not close keeps its residual and its reading is NOT adjusted to make the total come out — the residual is the finding.",
