 "closes": false,
 "closes_note": "MANUFACTURES AND TRADES CLOSES EXACTLY: seven single strokes on lines 2, 3, 5, 6, 13, 27 and 28 against a printed 7, and it is that column's ink the line grid was fitted to. Mining, agriculture, commerce, navigation of the ocean, navigation of canals lakes and rivers and the learned professions carry no mark on any of the 31 lines and no figure at the foot. Three marks in the industry block were examined at magnification and rejected as figures, and they are recorded rather than silently dropped: a broad feathered smear at y≈726 that crosses the printed rule at x'1898 between navigation of the ocean and navigation of canals (a figure does not cross a rule; it is an offset transfer or a stain), a round ink blot at y≈1068 straddling the same kind of rule at x'1817, and a compact 15 px spot at y≈1301 in navigation of canals that carries none of this hand's stroke.\n\nTHE FAMILY TOTALS READ 114 AGAINST A PRINTED 133 — a residual of -19, and nothing was altered to make it smaller. The reading is 31 single digits and the shortfall cannot be absorbed by a missed line or a missed tens figure: the connected-component pass finds exactly 31 glyph groups, no slot empty and no group wider than 76 px against a 193 px column, so there is no thirty-second household and no two-figure number read as one. Where the nineteen persons are cannot be said. The inventory calls this sheet `filled but very faint` and that is the honest difficulty: the ink on lines 1 and 24 is broken — line 1's loop survives only in fragments above a long descender, and line 24 is a bold head with a hairline tail where the pen ran dry — and either could carry a larger figure than the 9 and the 1 committed here. No cell was re-read after the sum was known, and no line's figure was raised to close the column.\n\nThe digit forms were fixed against 33S7-9YYJ-B1, an adjacent leaf in the same hand whose own TOTAL column closes to one person, and its labelled 3, 5, 6, 9, 4, 2 and 1 were laid beside this sheet's glyphs at 9x before each was committed — see `digit_key_used`.",
 "pairing": {
